Z||000003||GeeDee86GT||12-05-2000||06:41 PM||||I'm in the same boat.  My '84 parts car has nice wheels but locked.  The car is seized. No welder, round nuts, no locks. I've decided to drill out the stud from the outside as I don't care about any parts except the wheels. I considered a hole saw a bit larger than the center of the stud to get rid of some of the thread to thread contact. Then an air hammer to drive the stud out the back. ???? <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/garby_d" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/garby_d</A> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/garby_d/siggy.jpg"> ||||reg||8
Z||000004||theogre||12-06-2000||09:49 PM||||There's an article about the OE locks and where to order keys and such on the NIFE (Northern IL Fiero something.) club's site. I can't find the stupid link right now.<P>For aftermarket locks is depends partly who made them. The cheap ones are a little softer. The thing to do would be tack weld something to the lock on most of them. You can't get room to clamp them on Fiero stock wheels like you can on some cars.<P>Another posibility would be to rough the hell out of them and epoxy something to them. The more you could chew them up the happier the epoxy would be. You also want to clean them with brake cleaner or alcohol to get rid of any oil. I haven't tried this one but todays epoxies are so strong I can't see why it shouldn't work. Main thing is getting stuff clean so there's nothing keeping it from bonding.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||1
